---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import OrbStage from '../../components/OrbStage.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Lab · Editorial · Niyora"
  description="Experimental editorial layout preview. Not production."
  canonicalUrl="https://niyora.com/lab/home-editorial"
>
  <OrbStage />

  <div class="lab-label" aria-hidden="true">Lab: Editorial</div>

  <main class="ed">
    <!-- Hero: type pushed hard to the left, orb breathes in the empty right. -->
    <section class="ed-hero" data-hue="335" data-sat="58%" data-orb-alpha="0.9" data-orb-scale="0.9">
      <p class="kicker">Niyora</p>
      <h1>Life doesn't wait<br />for PMS.<br /><span class="muted">But we do.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">Niyora is a PMS app that sees your week coming. When feelings run high, it helps you reflect, regulate, and respond, instead of react.</p>
      <div class="hero-foot">
        <button type="button" class="btn">I'm interested</button>
        <p class="fine">Free · No account · No wearables · No cycle data shared</p>
        <p class="fine strong">Your personal details never reach the AI.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- One fact: a single oversized statement, ragged right. -->
    <section class="ed-fact" data-hue="330" data-sat="55%">
      <p class="index">01</p>
      <p class="eyebrow">Built on one fact</p>
      <h2 class="stmt">Women with emotional regulation skills handle PMS better.</h2>
      <p class="lede offset">That's the whole idea. Staying steady is a skill, not something you're born with, and skills can be learned. Niyora teaches it a little at a time, so the week gets easier to carry.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem: heading left, research notes as a ragged right column. -->
    <section class="ed-split" data-hue="300" data-sat="55%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">02</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">What no one told you</p>
        <h2>Your hormones are normal. Your brain just feels the shift more.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The difference between a hard PMS week and an easy one isn't your hormone levels. It's how strongly your brain responds to the same normal rise and fall. Two in three women feel it as mood swings.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="notes">
        <li>
          <h3>Same hormones, different response.</h3>
          <p>When researchers measured them, women who feel awful before their period had the same hormone levels as women who feel fine. The whole difference is sensitivity.</p>
          <span class="cite">NIMH · Schmidt &amp; Rubinow; Hantsoo &amp; Payne, 2020</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>It's wired in, and it runs in families.</h3>
          <p>Premenstrual sensitivity is about 56 percent heritable. The same shift other people barely notice, your system actually feels. A bit like having sensitive skin.</p>
          <span class="cite">Dubey et al, Molecular Psychiatry, 2017</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>It has an end date.</h3>
          <p>When researchers switched these hormones off, the symptoms cleared. When they switched them back on, they returned. It rolls in, and it rolls out, every cycle.</p>
          <span class="cite">NIMH GnRH add-back studies</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Solution: three steps as a numbered editorial run, each full-width row. -->
    <section class="ed-steps" data-hue="278" data-sat="55%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">03</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Moon AI · during PMS</p>
        <h2>Reflect. Regulate. Respond.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-n">1</span>
        <div>
          <p class="step-tag">Reflect</p>
          <h3>See it more than one way.</h3>
          <p>Tell Moon what happened. When you're upset, your mind locks onto one story. Moon shows you a few other ways to see it, so that one stops feeling like the truth.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-n">2</span>
        <div>
          <p class="step-tag">Regulate</p>
          <h3>Get through the twenty minutes.</h3>
          <p>A big wave of feeling takes about twenty minutes to pass. Moon gives you one small thing to do with your body to get through it, so it doesn't take over.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-n">3</span>
        <div>
          <p class="step-tag">Respond</p>
          <h3>Send the one you won't regret.</h3>
          <p>Worked up, it's easy to say something you'll wish you hadn't. Moon writes a few ways you could reply, so you pick the one you'll feel good about, and actually send it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Training: heading left, cards ragged right. -->
    <section class="ed-split" data-hue="258" data-sat="58%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">04</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Training · before PMS</p>
        <h2>Build the skill before you need it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">It's easier to stay steady when you've practised it calm. Short, playful lessons show you how to handle different feelings, so the skill is ready when you need it.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="notes">
        <li><h3>Handle the sharp email.</h3><p>Learn to meet a hard meeting or a pointed message without it costing you the whole day.</p><span class="cite">At work</span></li>
        <li><h3>Keep a small thing small.</h3><p>Read a moment before you react, so a passing friction doesn't grow into something it wasn't.</p><span class="cite">Between people</span></li>
        <li><h3>Name it without a fight.</h3><p>Practise telling the difference between PMS and a real issue, and bringing it up so it lands well.</p><span class="cite">With your partner</span></li>
        <li><h3>Learn from how others handled it.</h3><p>Work through real PMS moments and the way each was handled, so the lesson sticks better than any rule.</p><span class="cite">Real stories</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Relationships. -->
    <section class="ed-split" data-hue="320" data-sat="58%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">05</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Couples</p>
        <h2>Is it PMS, or is it real? Either way, Niyora helps.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The week before your period, your reserves run thin and small things land sharp. It isn't that you love anyone less. When you steady yourself first, there's less to spill onto the people you care about.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="notes">
        <li><h3>Know which one you're in.</h3><p>A quick read on whether the feeling is the week talking or a real thing to raise. Both are valid. Knowing which changes what you do next.</p><span class="cite">PMS or real?</span></li>
        <li><h3>A message, already written.</h3><p>Tips and ready-to-send lines for the hard conversations, so a plain ask is easier to meet than a mood to read.</p><span class="cite">Say it well</span></li>
        <li><h3>Come back from it.</h3><p>If it blew up, Niyora hands you a short, honest message to reopen it. One rough moment isn't the whole story.</p><span class="cite">After a fight</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Compare: two ragged columns, no boxes, a hairline between. -->
    <section class="ed-compare" data-hue="230" data-sat="60%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">06</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why not just use ChatGPT</p>
        <h2>A frontier model keeps you talking. Moon helps you need it less.</h2>
        <p class="lede">General AI is built to hold your attention. That is the opposite of what a hard PMS moment needs. Here is the difference, line by line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cmp">
        <div class="cmp-col them">
          <p class="cmp-label">Any frontier model</p>
          <p>Built to keep the conversation going. It optimizes for time spent.</p>
          <p>Agrees with you. That is what keeps you talking.</p>
          <p>Lets you vent for as long as you like. Research says venting often makes it worse.</p>
          <p>Gives endless options and keeps going.</p>
          <p>Learns a lot about you, and is built to sound like a friend. That builds dependence.</p>
          <p>Doesn't know your PMS, and rarely raises how your actions land on others.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cmp-col us">
          <p class="cmp-label">Moon in Niyora</p>
          <p>Built to finish the loop, then stop. It optimizes for you needing it less.</p>
          <p>Shows you the other side on purpose. On your side, even when that means slowing you down.</p>
          <p>Helps you regulate first, so you respond with something you won't regret.</p>
          <p>Gets you to one response you can stand behind, then stops.</p>
          <p>No sign in, so you are never identifiable. It never calls itself human.</p>
          <p>Built for your PMS week, and keeps the people around you in view.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="lede close">No ads, no profile, nothing sold. There is no version of Niyora that makes money from your worst moment.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Research: heading left, papers as a dense ragged list. -->
    <section class="ed-split" data-hue="248" data-sat="62%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">07</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Research backed</p>
        <h2>Over 30 science-backed activities. Every one has a paper behind it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">No mystic wellness talk. Just practices drawn from peer-reviewed research, cited so you can read the source yourself. Niyora also recognises how strong your PMS is with a short test, and points to the lifestyle changes that actually move the needle.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="papers">
        <li><b>Differential hormone sensitivity.</b> Schmidt et al, NEJM. PMDD brains react to normal hormone levels.</li>
        <li><b>The heritable trait.</b> Dubey et al, 2017. Sensitivity is partly in your genes.</li>
        <li><b>GABA and the calm brake.</b> Allopregnanolone shifts your built-in calm before your period.</li>
        <li><b>Progressive muscle relaxation for PMS.</b> RCT. A large effect on premenstrual symptoms.</li>
        <li><b>Calcium for PMS.</b> Thys-Jacobs crossover RCT. One of the strongest nutrient findings.</li>
        <li><b>Box Breathing.</b> Used in Navy SEAL training. Lowers cortisol, improves HRV.</li>
        <li><b>4-7-8 Breath.</b> Activates the parasympathetic nervous system. Weil, A.</li>
        <li><b>5-4-3-2-1 Grounding.</b> Linehan, 1993 (DBT). Reduces panic and dissociation.</li>
        <li><b>Self-Compassion.</b> Neff, 2003. Linked to reduced anxiety and rumination.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine">and more, every card in Niyora traces back to a source.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Privacy. -->
    <section class="ed-split" data-hue="210" data-sat="60%">
      <div class="split-head">
        <p class="index">08</p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Private by design</p>
        <h2>Built to know almost nothing about you.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Your cycle and your feelings are the most personal data there is. Niyora is built so almost none of it reaches anyone, including the AI. Moon uses AI to write its reflections, so we're plain about exactly what it can and can't see.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="notes">
        <li><h3>No sign in</h3><p>No account, no login. Nothing you write is ever tied to your name.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Your details never reach the AI</h3><p>Before your words go to Moon's AI, names and identifying details are stripped on your phone. What is left can't point back to you.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Nothing sold, nothing kept</h3><p>No ads, no profile, no data sold. Your period dates stay on your device, and are never shared.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Founder: a broadsheet pull-quote. -->
    <section class="ed-founder" data-hue="335" data-sat="60%">
      <p class="index">09</p>
      <p class="eyebrow">Why I built Niyora</p>
      <blockquote>I went looking for a way through PMS that didn't ask me to change my body.</blockquote>
      <div class="founder-body">
        <img src="/neha.jpg" alt="Neha Prasad" loading="lazy" />
        <div>
          <p>I'm a doctor, so I started where I trust: the research. And the pattern was hard to miss. Most of what's offered for PMS either medicates it or just tracks it. Very little helps you in the actual hard hour.</p>
          <p>Then I found the quieter, stronger thread. The most reliable, least invasive way through a hard PMS week is emotion regulation. Learning to name what you feel, settle your body, and see the thought from a step back. No hormones to take, no device to wear, no data to hand over.</p>
          <p>That finding is the whole foundation of Niyora. Everything in the app is one small rep of emotion regulation, done in about a minute. I built it for myself first. Now it's yours.</p>
          <p class="sig">Neha Prasad. Doctor, builder, founder of Niyora.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Final CTA. -->
    <section class="ed-final" data-hue="30" data-sat="70%">
      <h2>Want Niyora when you need it?</h2>
      <p class="lede">Tap to say you're interested. No email, no account, nothing to sign. Just a nudge that tells us to keep building.</p>
      <button type="button" class="btn">I'm interested</button>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href="/lab/">← back to the lab</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Editorial: everything left-aligned, big expressive type, ragged columns,
     minimal glass. The mold-breaker is the asymmetry, not decoration. */
  .ed {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    max-width: 1180px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 clamp(20px, 6vw, 96px);
  }
  .ed section {
    padding: clamp(80px, 14vh, 160px) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
  }
  .ed p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); max-width: 60ch; }

  .kicker, .eyebrow {
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 50%, 76%, 0.75);
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    transition: color 1.4s ease;
  }
  .index {
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(52px, 11vw, 132px);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.97);
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }
  h1 .muted { color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 45%, 74%, 0.85); transition: color 1.4s ease; }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 56px);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
    max-width: 16ch;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  h3 { font-size: 19px; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92); margin-bottom: 8px; }

  .lede { font-size: clamp(18px, 2.1vw, 23px); font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.5; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.74); }
  .lede.offset { margin-left: auto; max-width: 42ch; }
  .lede.close { margin-top: 48px; font-size: 19px; }
  .fine { font-size: 14px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); margin-top: 10px; }
  .fine.strong { color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 60%, 82%, 0.95); font-weight: 500; transition: color 1.4s ease; }

  /* Hero: text hugs the left third, orb owns the negative space at right. */
  .ed-hero { max-width: 62%; }
  .hero-foot { margin-top: 44px; }

  .ed-fact .stmt {
    font-size: clamp(34px, 6.4vw, 92px);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 18ch;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.97);
  }

  /* Split: heading column and a ragged notes column, deliberately uneven. */
  .ed-split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr; gap: clamp(32px, 6vw, 96px); align-items: start; }
  .ed-split .split-head { position: sticky; top: 96px; }
  .notes { list-style: none; }
  .notes li { padding: 26px 0; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1); }
  .notes li:first-child { border-top: none; padding-top: 0; }
  .notes p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .cite { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.4px; color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 50%, 75%, 0.8); transition: color 1.4s ease; }

  /* Steps: full-width numbered rows. */
  .ed-steps .split-head { margin-bottom: 24px; }
  .step { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: clamp(24px, 6vw, 80px); padding: 40px 0; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1); align-items: baseline; }
  .step-n { font-size: clamp(64px, 12vw, 150px); font-weight: 200; line-height: 0.8; color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 45%, 70%, 0.45); transition: color 1.4s ease; }
  .step-tag { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase; color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 60%, 78%, 0.85); margin-bottom: 8px; transition: color 1.4s ease; }
  .step h3 { font-size: clamp(22px, 3vw, 34px); font-weight: 400; }

  /* Compare: two ragged text columns, hairline divider, zero boxes. */
  .cmp { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 6vw, 72px); margin-top: 12px; }
  .cmp-col { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px; }
  .cmp-col p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; padding-bottom: 18px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); }
  .cmp-label { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase; border-bottom: none !important; padding-bottom: 0 !important; }
  .cmp-col.them p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.48); }
  .cmp-col.them .cmp-label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4); }
  .cmp-col.us p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82); }
  .cmp-col.us .cmp-label { color: hsla(var(--orb-hue), 60%, 80%, 0.95); transition: color 1.4s ease; }

  .papers { list-style: none; columns: 2; column-gap: clamp(28px, 6vw, 72px); }
  .papers li { break-inside: avoid; padding: 14px 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6); border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }
  .papers b { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9); font-weight: 500; }

  /* Founder: broadsheet pull-quote then a two-column read. */
  .ed-founder blockquote {
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 64px);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin: 8px 0 44px;
  }
  .founder-body { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 5vw, 64px); align-items: start; }
  .founder-body img { width: 128px; height: 128px; border-radius: 50%; object-fit: cover; border: 2px solid hsla(var(--orb-hue), 50%, 70%, 0.35); box-shadow: 0 0 30px hsla(var(--orb-hue), 60%, 55%, 0.3); transition: border-color 1.4s ease, box-shadow 1.4s ease; }
  .founder-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .sig { font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 0.5px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45) !important; }

  .ed-final { border-bottom: none; }
  .ed-final h2 { margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .ed-final .btn { margin-top: 28px; }

  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 16px 40px;
    border-radius: 32px;
    font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 1.5px solid transparent;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(rgba(18, 13, 26, 0.92), rgba(18, 13, 26, 0.92)),
      linear-gradient(95deg, #e6a3c4, #9c5c86, #7846b0);
    background-origin: border-box;
    background-clip: padding-box, border-box;
    box-shadow: 0 0 34px rgba(217, 146, 176, 0.26);
    transition: transform 0.3s ease, box-shadow 0.3s ease;
  }
  .btn:hover { transform: translateY(-1px); box-shadow: 0 0 46px rgba(217, 146, 176, 0.45); }

  .back { padding: 60px 0 100px; }
  .back a { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); text-decoration: none; }
  .back a:hover { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9); }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .ed-hero { max-width: 100%; }
    .ed-split, .cmp, .founder-body, .step { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .ed-split .split-head { position: static; }
    .papers { columns: 1; }
    .step-n { font-size: 72px; }
  }

  .lab-label {
    position: fixed;
    left: 16px;
    bottom: 16px;
    z-index: 50;
    padding: 8px 14px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
    background: rgba(34, 27, 54, 0.6);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    backdrop-filter: blur(16px);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(16px);
  }
</style>
